so that I can scarcely appeal to an example in illustration of my meaning with any hope that it will prove such to more than a very small portion of my hearers.

When Oken, after recognising in the scapula an aggregate of cervical ribs, afterwards defined the limbs as * liberated ribs,’ we perceive that neither his notion of ribs, or of the scapular arch, or of limbs, could be clear. When Carus explains the nature of limbs by calling them ‘tertiary vertebra,’ we discern the same obscurity in his idea of the primary segments of the skeleton. According to my definition of a vertebra, we recognise

in it the centrum, the neural arch, the haemal arch, and

the appendages diverging or radiating from the hzemal arch;

